313:
(a) When a dangerous animal or vicious animal is classified as such or when a vicious animal is reclassified as a dangerous animal, the animal control officer shall notify the animal's owner of such classification.
(b) Notices to the owner shall meet the following requirements:
(1) The notice shall be in writing and either mailed by certified mail to the owner's last known address or hand-delivered to the owner. Such notice shall be complete either upon its mailing or, if hand-delivered, upon personal receipt;
(2) The notice shall include a summary of the animal control officer's findings that formed the basis for the animal's classification or reclassification as a dangerous or vicious animal;
(3) The notice shall be dated and shall state that the owner, within 15 days after the date shown on the notice, has a right to request a hearing on the animal control officer's determination that the animal is a dangerous or vicious animal;
(4) The notice shall state that the hearing, if requested, shall be before the city or its designee and shall specify the name of the agency which shall conduct the hearing;
(5) The notice shall state that either a filing fee in the amount of $250.00 or a sufficient pauper's affidavit showing that the owner is unable without undue financial hardship to pay the filing fee shall be filed with the hearing request to the city or its designee for said hearing;
(6) The notice shall state that, if a hearing is not requested, the animal control officer's determination that an animal is a dangerous or vicious animal will become effective for all purposes under this chapter on a date specified in the notice, which shall be after the last day on which the owner has a right to request a hearing; and
(7) The notice shall include a form to request a hearing before the city or its designee and shall provide specific instructions on mailing or delivering such request to the governing designee.
(Ord. No. 2013-CO-10-717, § 5, 12-9-2013)

319:
Subject to the additional requirements of section 10-108 of this chapter, the animal control officer shall issue a certificate of registration to the owner of a dangerous or vicious animal if the owner complies with the following requirements including presenting to the animal control officer or the animal control officer otherwise finding sufficient evidence of:
(1) The posting of the premises where the dangerous animal or vicious animal is located with a clearly visible sign of the type specified by animal control warning that there is a dangerous animal on the property and containing a symbol designed to inform children of the presence of a dangerous animal. Said signs shall be exempt from any requirements for signs under this Code but, in no event, shall the signs be placed on the right of way of any road.
(2) A proper enclosure to confine the dangerous animal or vicious animal as per the following requirements:
a. All proper enclosures located outdoors shall:
1. Have a securely attached top;
2. Have the fence bottom embedded no less than 24 inches in the ground or attached to a concrete or asphalt floor;
3. Be constructed of no less than six gauge wire for the sides of the fence;
4. Have tubing that the fence is attached to no less than 18 gauges thick;
5. Be no smaller than 12 feet in length and 12 feet in width, although the height and any further width and length of the enclosure are left to the owner's discretion;
6. Provide protection from the elements for the animal;
7. Have only one gate that shall be of the inward opening type that is kept locked by key or combination lock except when the owner is inside the proper enclosure for the purpose of cleaning the kennel, providing food and water or otherwise tending to the animal; and
8. Be inside a perimeter or area fence of the property on which the proper enclosure is located.
b. Any structure other than a residence approved as a proper enclosure shall meet all other building codes as well as the following:
1. Have a securely attached top;
2. Provide protection from the elements for the animal; and
3. Not have any holes or other means of escape in walls, floors, windows, doors, ceiling, vents and all walls, floors, windows, doors, vents and ceiling are securely attached to the frame of the building.
c. A proper enclosure shall not include the owner's residence with the exception of a proper enclosure for a vicious animal that is 25 lbs. or under in which event the proper enclosure may be comprised of the following:
1. Have a securely attached top;
2. Be a kennel of no less than five-foot in length by five-foot in width and five foot in height located inside the residence;
3. Have a locked gate that shall remain locked by key or combination lock except when the owner is inside the proper enclosure for the purpose of cleaning the kennel, providing food and water or otherwise tending to the animal;
4. Have six gauge wire fencing;
5. Have tubing that shall not be less than 18 gauges thick; and
6. Be allowed only if the owner and the owner of the property on which the proper enclosure is located consent to allow animal control to inspect the inside of the residence where the proper enclosure is maintained or any other area where the animal is found randomly and without notice for compliance with maintaining the animal in the proper enclosure.
(3) It shall be the duty of every owner of a dangerous or vicious animal to have the animal permanently identified by insertion of a microchip by Hall County Animal Services and pay the required fee for the insertion of the microchip in accordance with the chapter 22 of this Code.
(4) Consent from the owner of the animal and the owner of the property that animal control shall have the right to inspect, randomly and without notice, a dangerous animal or vicious animal on the property of the person to whom the certificate of registration is issued to include its proper enclosure, if the animal is therein, or the entire property until the animal is found if the animal is not in the proper enclosure for the purpose of assuring compliance with this chapter.
(Ord. No. 2013-CO-10-717, § 5, 12-9-2013)

329:
(a) A vicious animal shall be immediately confiscated by the animal control officer, by a law enforcement officer or by another person authorized by the animal control officer if:
(1) The owner of the animal does not secure and maintain the liability insurance or bond required by this chapter;
(2) The animal is not validly registered as required by this chapter;
(3) The animal is not maintained in the proper enclosure approved by animal control;
(4) The premises where the animal is located are not posted as required by this chapter; or
(5) The animal is outside the proper enclosure approved by animal control in violation of this chapter.
(b) A dangerous animal shall be confiscated in the same manner as a vicious animal if:
(1) The animal is not validly registered as required by this chapter;
(2) The animal is not maintained in the proper enclosure approved by animal control;
(3) The premises where the animal is located are not posted as required by this chapter; or
(4) The animal is outside the proper enclosure approved by animal control in violation of this chapter.
(c) Any animal that has been confiscated under the provisions of this section shall be returned to its owner upon the owner's compliance with the provisions of this chapter and upon the payment of all required impoundment, boarding, veterinary and other fees set forth in chapter 22 of this Code. In the event the owner has not complied with the provisions of this chapter within ten business days of the date the animal was confiscated, said animal shall be destroyed in an expeditious and humane manner.
(Ord. No. 2013-CO-10-717, § 6, 12-9-2013)
